summary.value:
This paper proposed a novel algorithm which estimates the Wasserstein distance between two datasets (held by two different parties) without leaking too much information to the other party, such that one party cannot reconstruct the data distribution of the other party. The high level idea of the proposed algorithm is inspired by similar triangles: Let two datasets considered be \mu and 
u, we pick an arbitrary dataset \gamma, if \eta_{\mu}(t) is a linear interpolation between \mu and \gamma (i.e., t * \mu + (1-t) * \gamma), and \eta_{
u}(t) is defined similarly, then (distance between \eta_{\mu}(t) and \eta_{
u}(t))/t is a good approximation to the distance between \mu and 
u. The paper also shows that if two parties use different t, how do they get the approximation. In addition, the paper also proves that: given the information, the dataset distribution of one party is impossible to be reconstructed by the other.

